  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Disorganized area of low pressure is forming\nalong the offshore frontal boundary. There continues to be high\nconfidence that the offshore low will develop into a potential\nnor'easter and strengthen while lingering well off the Carolina\ncoast through mid week. Strong winds, high waves, and coastal \nflooding as well as extremely dangerous marine conditions are\nexpected. What is more uncertain is where the eventual track and\nstrength of the low will be beyond Tuesday, as is often the case\nwith a low pressure system that has not become organized yet. \n\nAs the low finally becomes to take shape and organize over the\nnext 24 hrs, a better handle on where and when it tracks will \nbecome more certain. There is some concern for the low to deepen\nsomewhat rapidly and shift further west, to a position that is \nnot so far off the OBX. This bears some weight, as a cluster of \nsolutions from the AIFS Ens indicate, but is a lower likelihood \nat this point. Some of the CAMs continue to hint at this \nsolution and would lead to higher winds, potentially in the \nstorm force range Tue into mid week. For now have cont to \nforecast a middle-of- the-road solution.\n\nGusty winds cont on the OBX, and have cont the wind adv here.\nHave dropped the wind adv for Mainland Dare and Downeast\nCarteret, as the stronger winds will be confined to the OBX\ngoing forward. \n\n2-4 foot above ground level coastal flooding will be possible\nwith minor to moderate impacts throughout the Outer Banks, Core\nBanks and Downeast Carteret County. Higher values will be on the\nOBX ocean side, whereas the lower end values on the soundside. \n\nAdditionally, a high surf advisory continues for Cape Lookout\nand points north through the OBX. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass that has advected in\nwill remain with us through at least mid week with temps a bit \nbelow climo. Maximum temperatures through the period are \nexpected to remain in the 60-70 degree range. Overnight lows \nwill range from the 40s inland to the 50s coast. \n\nAs the low meanders offshore, some wrap around rain showers may\noccasionally sneak back onshore, with best chances on the OBX \nover the next couple of days.",
